for the past 160-ish years virginia has  been going through a bit of a breakup  as you know west virginia broke away  from the state during the american civil  war and yet even when the war was over  the two  never reunited which raises the question  why  why didn't the virginias get back  together so to begin when the civil war  broke out many in virginia did not wish  to join the confederacy as its leaders  in richmond had declared  as such a couple of conventions were  called in wheeling which first  established a brand new union recognized  government for virginia led by francis  pierpont and second called for the  immediate secession of these western  territories from virginia and the  creation of a new state  and that new state was to be known as  kanoha it wasn't long before delegates  chose the name west virginia instead  normally breaking away from a state  would be impossible since the state's  government would need to agree to the  separation  but since pierpont was seen as the  legitimate leader of all of virginia  that wasn't a problem the problem came  with the necessary referendum on west  virginia's new constitution and  existence as a state  the issue was that many areas within the  new state to be had supported secession  and were home to many slave owners who  were not too keen on joining the  abolitionist union  when the vote was held many areas were  occupied by union troops which in the  eyes of many affected the outcome  the areas polled voted to approve the  new constitution and to become a brand  new state  mostly because those opposed boycotted  the vote and soon after this these areas  were transferred to west virginia by  pierpont's government  this wasn't simply done for kicks but  because these areas have been listed  under the constitution as potential  parts of the state  in 1863 these two counties were further  added giving west virginia its  modern-day borders  and in the same year lincoln and  congress agreed to west virginia  becoming a state and it formally joined  the usa  so spoiler alert the union won the war  and after the confederacy's defeat there  were some that raised the question of  what was to be done with virginia and  west virginia  in 1866 the virginian government now  back in richmond asked its west  virginian opposite if it wanted to be  reincorporated back into virginia proper  to which the leaders of west virginia  shockingly said no  so that's all sorted right well no  whilst virginia couldn't regain all of  west virginia it hoped for the return of  some territory specifically these two  counties  and there were two main arguments for  this the first was that congress hadn't  approved the county's transfer until  1866 which was after the virginian  government had returned to richmond in  1865 and repealed most of pierpont's  government's legislation  which meant that congress had approved  the transfer of land from one state to  another without the consent of both  states  the second argument was that these  counties were occupied by union troops  instead of allowing a vote simply sent  delegates to wheeling to say yes and  thus it wasn't legitimate  this went to the supreme court in 1870  who the next year rejected virginia's  claim on the basis of you were rebelling  so shut up  the court ruling against virginia marked  the final attempt to change the status  of the two states  the usa would be left with two virginias  i hope you enjoyed this episode and  thank you for watching with a special  thanks to my patrons  james bissonette kelly moneymaker danny  maloney marvin castell the pastry  section rob waterhouse moe aaron the  white james castaneda jordan longley  gustav swan marcus asner jerry lamdin  brasheed ali cooling castleman spilling  three plates david silverman maggie  paxkowski coppertone  izzy spencer lightfoot phobojo matthew  shipley robert wetzel lexi schwinn and  winston caywood
 

